From b4e4621af6d07da6cf09b72e708ec1849fa69bb6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: akapug <akapug@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 10:55:14 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 04/22] sycl: fewer ops per trit plane in the PTQ1_0 reorder
 mat-vec, and a one-wave split

Three changes, measured on an Arc Pro B70 with every weight read from memory
(8 distinct weights; the time includes the activation quantize):

- After w = v * 3 each 16-bit lane's high byte is the trit. For one or two
  columns w now goes to dp4a unmasked, against activation words whose bytes 0
  and 2 are zeroed and whose bytes 1 and 3 hold the even (or odd) elements:
  6 ops per plane of 4 weights instead of 8. From three columns the gathered
  trit word, shared by all columns, stays cheaper, so those keep it.
- A full row group's plane stride is a constant, so its 7 weight loads share
  one address instead of building 7 64-bit per-lane addresses per block.
- The sub-groups per row group target about 5/8 of the sub-groups the device
  holds at once, so the launch fits one wave. A launch just past a whole number
  of waves ran its tail on a fraction of the device: 5120x17408 split 2 (2176
  sub-groups against 2048 resident) took 44.5 us, split 1 38.4.

5120x17408 and 17408x5120 in turn, n = 1 / 2 / 4 / 8:
  40.7 / 42.6 / 48.6 / 66.1 -> 38.7 / 39.6 / 41.7 / 57.2 us per mat-vec
Each shape alone after: 5120x17408 38.4 / 39.7 / 42.8 / 59.5, 17408x5120
38.5 / 39.1 / 40.4 / 56.2 (the 608 GB/s bound is 32.1 us: n = 1 is 83 %,
n = 2 81-82 %). One mat-vec repeated, weight in L2: 5120x17408 n = 1 28.6 -> 22.7 us.
